[114.474689, "i", "cat > BUGFIX_SUMMARY.txt << 'EOF'\n=== TIMESTAMP CORRUPTION BUG - FIXED ===\n\nPROBLEM:\n- 40 out of 200 rows in merged.parquet had NaT (Not a Time) values in the recorded_at column\n- All NaT values were from sensor_c data\n\nROOT CAUSE:\n- transform.py had a hardcoded datetime format: \"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\"\n- sensor_c.csv used European date format: \"DD/MM/YYYY HH:MM:SS\" (e.g., 25/03/2024 14:30:00)\n- Other sensor CSVs used ISO format: \"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS\" (e.g., 2024-03-01 08:00:00)\n- The hardcoded format couldn't parse DD/MM/YYYY dates, causing them to become NaT\n- errors='coerce' silently converted unparseable dates to NaT instead of raising errors\n\nSOLUTION:\n- Modified parse_timestamps() function in transform.py\n- Changed from: format=\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S\"\n- Changed to: format='mixed'\n- This allows pandas (v2.0+) to automatically detect and parse multiple date formats\n\nVERIFICATION:\n- Re-ran full pipeline: extract.py -> transform.py -> load.py\n- Final output: 200 rows, 0 NaT values\n- All sensor_c timestamps now prope"]
[114.474802, "i", "rly parsed (e.g., 2024-03-25 14:30:00)\n- Date range: 2024-03-01 08:00:00 to 2024-03-25 17:45:00\n- All 5 sensors have 40 rows each\n\nFILES MODIFIED:\n- transform.py (backup saved as transform.py.bak)\nEOF\n"]
